---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';
import Interlude from '../../components/Interlude.astro';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Moon Phases', href: '/explore/moon-phases/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Moon Phases: working with the lunar cycle"
  description="The 29.5-day lunar cycle explained phase by phase: new moon, first quarter, full moon, last quarter, and how astrologers use the rhythm without turning it into a rulebook."
  path="/explore/moon-phases/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>Working with the moon phases</h1>
        <p class="lede">This is one of my favorite cycles because you can watch it happen. The Moon changes visibly night by night, and the whole pattern repeats in about a month. Astrology gives us one way to think with that rhythm, not a rulebook we have to obey.</p>
      </div>
      <img class="hero__img" src={ocean.seaStackMoonrise.src} srcset={ocean.seaStackMoonrise.srcset} sizes="100vw" alt={ocean.seaStackMoonrise.alt} width={ocean.seaStackMoonrise.width} height={ocean.seaStackMoonrise.height} fetchpriority="high" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>The cycle itself</h2>
      <p>
        A complete cycle from one new moon to the next — a <em>lunation</em> — takes about 29.5
        days. The phases come from the changing geometry among Sun, Moon, and Earth, which changes
        how much of the Moon's sunlit half we can see.
      </p>
      <p>
        Astrologers often divide that cycle into eight phases. Four turning points make an easy
        structure for understanding the rhythm.
      </p>

      <h2>The four turning points</h2>
      <h3>New moon</h3>
      <p>
        Sun and Moon meet at the same zodiacal longitude and the Moon is largely hidden from view.
        Astrologically, the new moon is often treated as the opening of a cycle: something beginning,
        gathering, or not yet fully visible.
      </p>
      <h3>First quarter</h3>
      <p>
        About a week later, the Moon forms a <a href="/explore/aspects/square/">square</a> to the
        Sun. Symbolically this is often read as the point where an emerging direction encounters
        friction and has to become more concrete.
      </p>
      <h3>Full moon</h3>
      <p>
        At the full moon, Sun and Moon oppose one another and the lunar face is fully illuminated.
        Astrologers often associate this phase with culmination, visibility, contrast, or something
        becoming easier to see.
      </p>
      <h3>Last quarter</h3>
      <p>
        The second square arrives as the cycle wanes. It is commonly read as a phase of reassessment,
        completion, release, or deciding what should continue into the next cycle.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.moonPhasesInWater} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Using the rhythm without being ruled by it</h2>
      <p>
        I like the lunar cycle as a framework for attention. A month is short enough to notice
        change and long enough for a question, project, or feeling to develop. You can use the
        phases as prompts: What is beginning? Where is friction appearing? What is becoming clear?
        What is ready to be released or revised?
      </p>
      <p>
        What I would not do is turn those prompts into laws. You do not have to launch at a new moon,
        finish at a full moon, or postpone your life because a phase is supposedly wrong for the
        task. If following the Moon makes you more attentive, wonderful. If it makes you anxious
        about doing something on the “wrong” day, the tool has stopped being useful.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Interlude
    image={ocean.currents}
    eyebrow="One month at a time"
    heading="The value is in noticing the cycle, not obeying it."
    body="The Moon gives you a visible rhythm to return to. What you notice within that rhythm is more interesting than following a list of rules."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Making it personal</h2>
      <p>
        A lunation becomes more specific when you place it into a natal chart: which house it falls
        in, which natal planets it aspects, and what else is active at the same time. Your own natal
        Moon and its phase add another layer. This is the difference between knowing the phase and
        understanding how that particular phase may belong in a particular life.
      </p>
      <p>
        See the <a href="/current-sky/">current new and full moons</a> with dates and signs, or read
        about <a href="/explore/planets/moon/">the Moon in the chart</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Where is this lunation landing for you?"
    body="The phase is shared. Its house, aspects, and place in the rest of your chart are personal."
    buttonLabel="Monthly Personal Transits"
    buttonHref="/readings/monthly-transits/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
